{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 25, :comments ["<b>When you come into your fellow's vineyard.</b> Scripture speaks of a hired worker — that the owner of the house hired him to prune his vineyard. And so the Rabbis, of blessed memory, interpreted: it says here ״כי תבא״ and it says below (Deuteronomy 24:15) ״ולא תבא עליו השמש״ — just as there Scripture speaks of a hired worker, so too here Scripture speaks of a hired worker. And this is what Onkelos translated: ארי תתגר — 'when you hire yourself out.' For if you say it refers to the entry of any person, you have left no living creature alone (i.e., everyone would be permitted to raid)." "<b>And you shall not put into your vessel.</b> That would be theft, and the Torah permitted only to satisfy the craving soul — for since he is occupied and toiling with its fruit, if he had no possibility to eat lawfully he would eat unlawfully; therefore He permitted ״כנפשך שבעך״ — 'according to the craving of your soul,' in the manner of what is written (Proverbs 13:25): ״צדיק אוכל לשובע נפשו״. And the same law applies to standing grain: he may pluck by hand from the stalks, but he may not swing a sickle."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 23, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:50:43.404590912Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}